Prototype is a container lifetime rule, not a general object-creation rule.

~~~java
@Component
class JobRunner {
    private final ObjectProvider<JobContext> contexts;
    JobRunner(ObjectProvider<JobContext> contexts) { this.contexts = contexts; }

    void run() {
        JobContext context = contexts.getObject(); // a new prototype resolution
    }
}
~~~

Without ObjectProvider, constructor injection would supply only one JobContext to this singleton. Prototype scope is uncommon for ordinary stateless services. It is suitable when each requested object carries temporary state and the caller has a clear ownership and cleanup model.

```java
@Service // singleton
public class BillingService {
    private final InvoiceGenerator generator; // injected ONCE at startup

    public BillingService(InvoiceGenerator generator) {
        this.generator = generator; // same instance reused forever
    }
}
```

```java
@Service
public class BillingService {
    private final ObjectProvider<InvoiceGenerator> provider;

    public BillingService(ObjectProvider<InvoiceGenerator> provider) {
        this.provider = provider; // solves the prototype-in-singleton trap
    }

    public byte[] createInvoice() {
        var generator = provider.getObject(); // a fresh instance each call
        return generator.generatePDF();
    }
}
```
ObjectProvider turns the singleton into a factory caller, so each request gets its own prototype instance instead of a shared one.

