The scoped proxy lets a short-lived request or session bean be injected into a long-lived singleton safely.

```java
@Service // singleton
public class TaskService {
    @Autowired
    private TaskProcessor processor; // one prototype, captured at startup

    public String handle(String task) {
        return processor.process(task); // the same instance for every task
    }
}
```

```java
@Service
public class TaskService {
    private final ObjectProvider<TaskProcessor> provider;

    public TaskService(ObjectProvider<TaskProcessor> provider) {
        this.provider = provider;
    }

    public String handle(String task) {
        return provider.getObject().process(task); // a fresh prototype per task
    }
}
```
ObjectProvider defers lookup to call time, so each task gets its own TaskProcessor instead of a shared one.

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Lookup;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Scope;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.util.UUID;

@Component
@Scope("prototype")
class RequestProcessor {
    private final String requestId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
    public String process(String data) {
        return "Request " + requestId + " processed: " + data;
    }
}

@Service
abstract class OrderService {
    public String handleOrder(String order) {
        return createProcessor().process(order); // a fresh instance each call
    }

    @Lookup // Spring overrides this to return a new prototype every call
    protected abstract RequestProcessor createProcessor();
}
```
